================================================================================
          SHIELDFLOW PROXY — PUBLISHING & ROUTER SETUP GUIDE
================================================================================

This guide provides the complete, step-by-step setup to publish the ShieldFlow 
Proxy on your local home network (LAN) and connect it with your router/devices 
for domain blocklisting.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 1: SHORTEST WAY TO PUBLISH THE PROXY (LAN / LOCAL SERVER)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Publishing the proxy makes it reachable by other devices on your home network.

Step 1: Build & Launch Proxy Server
-----------------------------------
Open PowerShell in the project directory and run:

  npm run build
  npx pm2 start dist/main.js --name "shield-proxy"

  # (Alternative without PM2):
  # npm run start:prod

When started, ShieldFlow runs two services simultaneously:
  - Port 3000 : Web Management Dashboard & PAC service (/proxy.pac)
  - Port 3128 : HTTP & HTTPS CONNECT Proxy Server


Step 2: Find Your Computer's LAN IP Address
-------------------------------------------
Run in PowerShell:

  ipconfig

Look for your active Wi-Fi or Ethernet adapter and copy the IPv4 Address:
  Example: 192.168.1.50


Step 3: Allow Inbound Traffic in Windows Firewall
-------------------------------------------------
Allow incoming connections on proxy port 3128 through Windows Firewall:

  netsh advfirewall firewall add rule name="ShieldFlow Proxy" dir=in action=allow protocol=TCP localport=3128

Your proxy is now published and active on your home network:
  - Proxy IP   : 192.168.1.50   (Replace with your PC's IP)
  - Proxy Port : 3128


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 2: HOW TO ADD THIS PROXY TO YOUR HOME ROUTER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

IMPORTANT NOTE ON CONSUMER ROUTERS:
Standard home routers (TP-Link, D-Link, Huawei, Netgear, ZTE) route Layer 3/4 
IP packets and do NOT provide a single "WAN HTTP Proxy" setting. 
Choose the method below that matches your network:

================================================================================
METHOD A: Router DHCP Option 252 (WPAD Auto-Discovery)
================================================================================
Recommended for routers supporting custom DHCP options. Automatically configures 
all connected phones and PCs without manual device setup.

1. Open your browser and go to your router gateway:
   http://192.168.1.1 (or http://192.168.0.1)

2. Log in and navigate to:
   DHCP Server Settings -> Advanced / DHCP Options

3. Add Option 252:
   - Option Code : 252
   - Value / URL : http://192.168.1.50:3000/proxy.pac

4. Save and reboot your router. Any client connecting to Wi-Fi will automatically 
   route matching traffic through the proxy.


================================================================================
METHOD B: Advanced Routers (OpenWrt / DD-WRT / MikroTik / AsusWRT)
================================================================================
If your router runs custom Linux firmware, redirect outbound HTTP/HTTPS traffic 
directly to your proxy IP and port using iptables firewall rules:

  # Redirect HTTP (Port 80)
  iptables -t nat -A PREROUTING -i br-lan -p tcp --dport 80 -j DNAT --to-destination 192.168.1.50:3128

  # Redirect HTTPS (Port 443)
  iptables -t nat -A PREROUTING -i br-lan -p tcp --dport 443 -j DNAT --to-destination 192.168.1.50:3128


================================================================================
METHOD C: Direct Device Wi-Fi Proxy Setup (Simplest & Universal)
================================================================================
Works on ANY standard home router without modifying router settings:

1. Connect your device (Phone, Tablet, Laptop, or PC) to home Wi-Fi.
2. Open Wi-Fi Network Settings -> Details / Modify Network -> Proxy Settings.
3. Switch Proxy from "None" to "Manual":
   - Proxy Hostname / IP : 192.168.1.50 (Your server IP)
   - Proxy Port          : 3128
4. Save settings.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 3: HOW TO STOP / DEACTIVATE THE PROXY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Stopping the Server Shuts Down Both Ports:
   In this project, ProxyService runs directly inside the NestJS app.
   Stopping NestJS (Ctrl + C in terminal or 'pm2 stop shield-proxy') 
   automatically terminates both Port 3000 and Port 3128.

2. Remember to Turn Off Proxy in Wi-Fi Settings:
   If the server is shut down while client devices still have the proxy enabled, 
   their internet will fail with ERR_PROXY_CONNECTION_FAILED.
   Switching Proxy back to "None" on the device immediately restores normal internet.

3. Remove Windows Firewall Rule (Optional Cleanup):
   If you no longer need port 3128 open:
   netsh advfirewall firewall delete rule name="ShieldFlow Proxy"


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 4: DOMAIN BLOCKLIST CONFIGURATION GUIDE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

File location: src/common/config/proxy.config.ts

Configure root domains without 'www.' prefix:

  const SOCIALMEDIA = [
      'instagram.com',
      'tiktok.com',
      'facebook.com',
  ];

  const SOCIALSITES = [
      'wisden.com',
      'arena.ai',
      'angularspace.com',
  ];

  export const BLOCKED_DOMAINS = [
      ...SOCIALMEDIA,
      ...SOCIALSITES
  ];

WHY USE ROOT DOMAINS:
DomainBlockService checks exact matches and subdomains ending with '.domain'.
Using 'instagram.com' automatically blocks:
  - instagram.com
  - www.instagram.com
  - api.instagram.com
  - l.instagram.com
Blocked requests immediately receive an HTTP 403 Forbidden and the connection drops.
